  definition: 'The label approval process is the regulatory procedure by which the TTB reviews and either approves or rejects alcoholic beverage label applications to ensure compliance with federal labeling standards before products enter interstate commerce.',
  plain_english: 'Getting a label approved is a multi-step process that every alcohol producer or importer must go through. First, you design your label to meet all federal requirements: correct product type designation, accurate alcohol content, government warning, and no prohibited claims. Then you submit the application through COLAs Online with your label images and product details. A TTB specialist reviews everything against the applicable regulations. If the label passes, you receive approval. If something is wrong, you get a "needs correction" notice explaining what to fix. Common rejection reasons include incorrect class/type designations, missing mandatory elements, misleading statements, or unapproved health claims. After correction, you resubmit and the review cycle repeats until the label is approved.',
