  category: 'TTB Basics',
  definition: 'Interstate commerce in the context of beverage alcohol regulation refers to the sale, shipment, or transportation of alcoholic beverages across state lines, which triggers federal regulatory requirements including COLA approval and federal permitting.',
  plain_english: 'Interstate commerce simply means selling or shipping products across state lines. In the alcohol world, this distinction is critically important because it determines which rules apply to you. If you only sell your products within the state where you make them, you may be able to use a Certificate of Exemption instead of a full COLA. But the moment you ship a single bottle to another state or sell to a distributor who operates in multiple states, you are engaged in interstate commerce and must have a full COLA for every product. This is why virtually every commercial alcohol product in the U.S. has a COLA, since very few producers limit themselves to selling only within their home state.',
