  definition: 'Beverage alcohol refers to any alcoholic drink intended for human consumption, including wine, distilled spirits, and malt beverages, as distinguished from industrial alcohol, denatured alcohol, or alcohol used in non-beverage products.',
  plain_english: 'Beverage alcohol is the industry term for any alcoholic drink meant for people to consume. This covers the three main categories regulated by the TTB: wine (made from grapes or other fruits), distilled spirits (whiskey, vodka, tequila, gin, rum, brandy, and others made through distillation), and malt beverages (beer, ale, and similar drinks made from malted grains). The term specifically excludes alcohol used for industrial purposes, medical products, cooking extracts, and other non-drinking uses. The beverage alcohol industry in the United States is one of the most heavily regulated consumer products sectors, with overlapping federal, state, and local regulations governing every step from production to retail sale.',
  technical_detail: 'Under federal law, beverage alcohol is broadly defined in the Internal Revenue Code (26 U.S.C. section 5002) and the FAA Act (27 U.S.C. section 211). The TTB classifies beverage alcohol into three regulatory categories, each governed by separate sections of the Code of Federal Regulations: wine (27 CFR Part 4), distilled spirits (27 CFR Part 5), and malt beverages (27 CFR Part 7). Tax rates differ significantly by category: distilled spirits are taxed at $13.50 per proof gallon (for the first 100,000 proof gallons), wine rates vary from $1.07 to $3.40 per gallon depending on type, and beer is taxed at $3.50 per barrel for the first 60,000 barrels for small brewers. These distinctions affect labeling requirements, permit types, and reporting obligations.',
