  plain_english: 'When you see "Reserve" on a wine label in America, it is essentially a marketing claim. Unlike in some European countries where "Reserva" or "Riserva" has a legal definition tied to specific aging requirements, the word "Reserve" has no legal meaning in the United States. A winery can put "Reserve" on any of their wines without meeting any particular quality or aging threshold. That said, many reputable wineries do use the term to genuinely distinguish their best products, reserving the designation for wine from their best vineyard blocks or longest-aged barrels. The challenge for consumers is that there is no way to know from the label alone whether "Reserve" indicates genuine quality selection or is simply marketing.',
  technical_detail: 'The TTB does not define or regulate the term "Reserve" for wine or spirits produced in the United States. This was confirmed in TTB Ruling 2005-1. The term may appear on labels as long as it is not used in a misleading context. In contrast, many other countries have specific legal definitions: Spain requires Reserva wines to be aged a minimum of 3 years (with at least 1 year in oak for reds), and Italy requires Riserva wines to meet specific aging minimums that vary by DOC/DOCG designation. For imported wines bearing "Reserve" or similar terms, the TTB evaluates whether the term complies with the laws of the country of origin.',
